  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause neck pain, along with a wide range of other symptoms. Here's a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd34 Direct Neck-Related Symptoms\n\n- A common symptom of parathyroid disease is a lump in the neck, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid.\n- A 45-year-old woman who presented with non-specific neck and shoulder pain was found to have mild hypercalcemia and markedly elevated parathyroid hormone levels, illustrating that neck pain can be a presenting symptom of parathyroid disease.\n- In later stages, tumors can grow large enough to be felt during daily activities like swallowing or talking.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddb4 Bone and Body Pain\n\n- The most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism are chronic fatigue, body aches, difficulty sleeping, bone pain, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches.\n- Other signs and symptoms can include pain in the abdomen, side, or back that doesn't go away, as well as pain in the bones.\n- Osteoporosis \u2014 a complication of parathyroid disease \u2014 can lead to broken bones and fractures. If one of these fractures happens in the spine or neck, it could even cause more serious complications.\n\n---\n\n### \u2697\ufe0f Why Does Parathyroid Disease Cause These Symptoms?\n\n- Occasionally, one or more parathyroid glands may make too much hormone, a condition known as **primary hyperparathyroidism**. When this happens, calcium is drawn out of the bones and dumped into the blood, where it can cause a number of problems.\n- Parathyroid disease also frequently leads to osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\ude7a Other Common Symptoms of Parathyroid Disease\n\n- Chronic fatigue and weakness\n- Kidney stones\n- Depression and difficulty concentrating\n- Constipation\n- Increased thirst and urination\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc8a Treatment\n\n- There is no medicine that can cure primary hyperparathyroidism. The only way to solve the problem is with an operation in which the abnormal parathyroid glands are removed.\n- Surgery should be considered by most patients, because the effects of primary hyperparathyroidism on the bones usually worsen with time.\n\n---\n\n**In summary**, neck pain in parathyroid disease can occur due to tumor growth in the neck region, bone deterioration, or \u2014 in rare cases like parathyroid cancer \u2014 direct tissue involvement. If you are experiencing unexplained neck pain along with symptoms like fatigue, bone pain, or kidney stones, it is worth speaking to your doctor about getting your calcium and parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels checked.",
